COMM(1)                        P32W Unix                         COMM(1)

NAME
       comm - lines in common to two sorted files

SYNOPSIS
       comm [-123] file1 file2

DESCRIPTION
       comm reads two files, which should be sorted (as by sort), and
       writes three columns: the lines only in file1, the lines only
       in file2, and the lines in both. Each column is indented by a
       tab for each column before it that is written. A file named -
       is the standard input. Lines may be of any length.

OPTIONS
       -1     leave out the lines only in file1
       -2     leave out the lines only in file2
       -3     leave out the lines in both
       Options may be combined: comm -12 writes only the lines in
       both, comm -23 only the lines only in file1.

EXAMPLES
       comm -12 old new              lines in both
       comm -13 old new              lines added in new

EXIT STATUS
       0; 1 for a usage error or if a file could not be opened.

SEE ALSO
       sort(1), uniq(1), join(1), cmp(1)

BUGS
       Lines are compared byte by byte, as sort compares them; if a
       file is not sorted the output is not useful.
